---
/*
 * The landing page.
 *
 * The order is the argument. The quick start sits above the case for the tool,
 * not below it: a reader who arrived already convinced — from the repo, from a
 * link, from someone who told them to use this — must not have to scroll past
 * persuasion to reach instructions. The problem section is for the reader who
 * is still deciding, and it is right there when the steps run out.
 *
 * Everything on this page is derived from ../README.md and ../docs/. Where the
 * source states a limit, the limit is stated here at the same strength.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import HeroDiagram from '../components/HeroDiagram.astro';
import FlowDiagram from '../components/FlowDiagram.astro';
import Quickstart from '../components/Quickstart.astro';
import PlatformTable from '../components/PlatformTable.astro';

/*
 * A `doctor` run over the config the quick start walks through.
 *
 * The shape is the tool's own: `<tag> [<check>] <message>`, with the checks it
 * actually emits — permissions, backend, secrets, ambient. `git` and `config`
 * findings are absent because those checks stay silent when nothing is wrong,
 * which is why a clean run is short.
 */
const doctor = `ok   [permissions] /Users/you/.config/gitwho and its files are owner-only
ok   [backend] secrets are in the age file at /Users/you/.config/gitwho/secrets.age, chosen by default
ok   [secrets] Personal/GH_TOKEN 4c1f9ab30e77
ok   [secrets] Work/GH_TOKEN 9e02b7d5164a
warn [ambient] GH_TOKEN is set in the environment; every process launched from this shell inherits it`;

const READING = [
  {
    href: '/docs/install/',
    label: 'docs/install',
    gloss: 'setting it up step by step, with a check after each one and the traps that pass falsely',
  },
  {
    href: '/docs/config/',
    label: 'docs/config',
    gloss: 'the annotated accounts.toml the tool itself ships, every field explained inline',
  },
  {
    href: '/docs/recipes/',
    label: 'docs/recipes',
    gloss: 'four complete configs, checked against that schema at build time',
  },
  {
    href: '/docs/design/',
    label: 'docs/design',
    gloss: 'why it is shaped this way: the resolution algorithm, the evidence, the threat model',
  },
  {
    href: '/docs/security/',
    label: 'docs/security',
    gloss: 'what the encryption defends against, and what it plainly does not',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/DanielCarmingham/gitwho',
    label: 'github.com/DanielCarmingham/gitwho',
    gloss: 'the source, the issue tracker, and the README this page is built from',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="gitwho — the right git identity, per repository"
  description="gitwho resolves your git identity and credentials from a repository's remote URL rather than its path on disk, so a worktree or a relocated clone still commits as the right person."
>
  <!-- 1. The idea, then the diagram that carries it. -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="prose">
      <h1>Identity follows the repository, not the directory.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        gitwho resolves your git identity and credentials from a repository's remote URL rather than
        where it sits on disk — so a worktree, a relocated clone or a temp directory still commits
        as the right person.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero__figure">
      <HeroDiagram />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Instructions before argument. -->
  <div class="section">
    <Quickstart />
  </div>

  <!-- 3. The case, for the reader still deciding. -->
  <section class="section prose">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Working across several accounts — personal, two employers, a self-hosted Gitea — means every
      repository needs a different author identity <em>and</em> different credentials for whatever
      touches it: <code>gh</code>, <code>tea</code>, an IDE, an MCP server. Getting it wrong is
      quiet. You commit as the wrong person, or authenticate as the wrong account, and nothing tells
      you.
    </p>
    <p>
      The usual approaches key off the <strong>filesystem path</strong> — <code
        >includeIf "gitdir:"</code
      > rules plus per-directory environment loading. That breaks as soon as a repository is not where
      the rule expects: a clone made somewhere else, or a worktree placed in a tool's own root. It breaks
      <em>silently</em>, falling back to whichever account is the default.
    </p>
    <p>
      So gitwho keys off the repository instead. Identity resolves from the remote URL, credentials
      come from a helper git hands the URL it is about to contact, and CLI credentials are injected
      into one process for one invocation. One file declares your accounts; nothing else needs
      editing when you add one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. What a run actually reports, warning included. -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="doctor">What <code class="bare">doctor</code> reports</h2>
      <p>
        <code>gitwho doctor</code> is the one to run after any change. It is read-only, exits
        non-zero on problems, and never prints a secret value — only fingerprints — so its output is
        safe to paste when you are asking for help.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doctor">
      <CodeBlock code={doctor} label="a healthy gitwho doctor run" />
    </div>
    <div class="prose doctor__note">
      <p>
        That <code>[ambient]</code> line is left visible on purpose. It means a provider token is
        sitting in the environment, where every process launched from that shell inherits it — which
        is the exposure this tool exists to remove, not a check to quieten. The README reports the
        same warning against the author's own machine rather than hiding it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. How a credential reaches exactly one process. -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>One process, one invocation</h2>
      <p>
        The property that matters is not the encryption. It is that a token is fetched by the one
        process that needs it, at the moment it needs it, instead of sitting in your environment
        where everything you launch inherits it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero__figure">
      <FlowDiagram />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. The limits, at the strength the README states them. -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Platform reality</h2>
      <p>
        Stated precisely, because the gap between these rows is easy to paper over.
      </p>
    </div>
    <PlatformTable />
    <div class="prose platform-note">
      <p>
        One more gap, in the tool rather than the platform: <code>jj</code> takes its credentials
        from git, so pushing works — but it keeps author identity in its own config and does not
        read gitconfig's <code>includeIf</code> rules, so in a colocated repository <code>jj</code>
        and <code>git</code> can commit as different people without saying so.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Where to go next. -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Reading</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="reading">
      {
        READING.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>
            <span>{item.gloss}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /*
   * The hero and the first step of the quick start have to share the first
   * screen on a laptop, so the space under the hero is one step tighter than
   * the rhythm the rest of the page keeps.
   */
  .hero__figure {
    margin-top: var(--s6);
  }

  .section {
    margin-top: var(--s8);
  }

  .hero + .section {
    margin-top: var(--s7);
  }

  /* Headings name a command here, and a command keeps its face without
     acquiring the inline-code chip in the middle of a heading. */
  h2 .bare {
    padding: 0;
    font-size: 1em;
    background: none;
    border: 0;
  }

  /*
   * Terminal output, not prose: measured, the longest line here is about 102
   * characters, so the reading column would put a scrollbar under a block whose
   * whole job is to be read at a glance. Wider than `--measure`, narrower than
   * the shell — the surrounding paragraphs stay in the reading column.
   */
  .doctor {
    margin-top: var(--s4);
    max-width: 60rem;
  }

  .doctor__note {
    margin-top: var(--s4);
  }

  .platform-note {
    margin-top: var(--s6);
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  /*
   * Destinations in mono, glosses in sans: the same split the header and
   * footer use. Two columns once there is room for the longest path, one
   * column below that rather than a wrapped and ragged pair.
   */
  .reading {
    margin-top: var(--s5);
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .reading li + li {
    margin-top: var(--s4);
    padding-top: var(--s4);
    border-top: var(--hair) solid var(--border);
  }

  .reading a {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
  }

  .reading span {
    display: block;
    margin-top: var(--s1);
    font-size: var(--t-md);
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .reading li {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 22rem 1fr;
      gap: var(--s4);
      align-items: baseline;
    }

    .reading span {
      margin-top: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
